---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/WithBlocksSidebar.astro';
import WebsiteHeader from '../../../components/blocks/WebsiteHeader.astro';
import WebsiteFooter from '../../../components/blocks/WebsiteFooter.astro';
import '../../../styles/blocks/site.scss';
import '../../../styles/blocks/blog-post.scss';

type Accent = 'blue' | 'green' | 'purple';

interface RelatedPost {
  title: string;
  topic: string;
  accent: Accent;
  author: string;
  date: string;
  dateLabel: string;
  image: string;
  width: number;
  height: number;
}

const rule = `{
  "table": "incident",
  "operation": "read",
  "roles": ["agent"],
  "condition": "assignment_group IN current_user.groups"
}`;

const related: RelatedPost[] = [
  {
    title: 'One customer, one database',
    topic: 'Engineering',
    accent: 'blue',
    author: 'Aiko Tanaka',
    date: '2026-10-14',
    dateLabel: '14 October 2026',
    image: '/assets/img/code-editor.webp',
    width: 492,
    height: 300,
  },
  {
    title: 'Designing forms that follow the table',
    topic: 'Product',
    accent: 'purple',
    author: 'Lena Fischer',
    date: '2026-09-30',
    dateLabel: '30 September 2026',
    image: '/assets/img/html-editor.webp',
    width: 1100,
    height: 600,
  },
  {
    title: 'How Fernhill Logistics left its spreadsheets behind',
    topic: 'Customers',
    accent: 'green',
    author: 'Priya Natarajan',
    date: '2026-10-21',
    dateLabel: '21 October 2026',
    image: '/assets/img/table.webp',
    width: 1166,
    height: 480,
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title='Blog post'
  description='A blog post built from Peacock components: the shared site header, a breadcrumb, a topic tag, the author with date and reading time, long-form text with a highlighted code sample and a quote, a sticky "On this page" anchor nav beside it, related post cards and the site footer.'
>
  <div class='block-demo site-demo blogpost-demo'>
    <WebsiteHeader current='blog' />

    <main class='site-main blogpost-main'>
      <wc-breadcrumb label='Breadcrumb'>
        <wc-breadcrumb-item href='#'>Blog</wc-breadcrumb-item>
        <wc-breadcrumb-item href='#'>Engineering</wc-breadcrumb-item>
        <wc-breadcrumb-item current>One check on every read and write</wc-breadcrumb-item>
      </wc-breadcrumb>

      <header class='blogpost-header'>
        <wc-tag accent='blue'>Engineering</wc-tag>
        <h2 class='text-display-small'>Why every read and write passes one check</h2>
        <p class='text-body-large site-muted'>
          Agents, customers, flows and the API all reach the same records. Here is why we let one place decide who may see and change them.
        </p>
        <div class='blogpost-byline'>
          <wc-avatar name='Jonas Lindqvist'></wc-avatar>
          <div class='blogpost-byline-text'>
            <span class='text-title-small'>Jonas Lindqvist</span>
            <span class='text-body-small site-muted'>
              <time datetime='2026-10-28'>28 October 2026</time> · 6 min read
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <figure class='blogpost-hero'>
        <img src='/assets/img/condition-builder.webp' alt='An access rule in the condition builder: assignment group is one of my groups' width='685' height='441' />
      </figure>

      <div class='blogpost-layout'>
        <article id='blogpost-body' class='site-prose blogpost-body text-body-large'>
          <p>
            Most business applications grow a second door. The forms check who you are, but a report, an export or an integration reads the
            tables directly. Sooner or later the two disagree, and someone sees a record they should not.
          </p>

          <h3 id='blogpost-problem' class='text-headline-small'>The problem with many checks</h3>
          <p>
            When every screen checks access on its own, every new screen is a new chance to get it wrong. Tests help, but they test the screens
            you thought of. The customer portal, the nightly import and the API each carry their own copy of the rules.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>A field hidden on the form still comes back from the API.</li>
            <li>An export runs as an administrator because that was easier.</li>
            <li>A flow updates records its starter could never open.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 id='blogpost-one-path' class='text-headline-small'>One data path</h3>
          <p>
            In Redvars there is one way to read or write a record: through the kernel. Forms, lists, rules, flows, the portal and the REST routes
            all go through it, and it runs the same check every time. There is no faster path around it, so there is nothing to forget.
          </p>
          <p>Access rules are records too. A rule names a table, an operation, the roles it applies to and an optional condition:</p>
          <wc-code-highlighter language='json' label='incident-read.json' value={rule}></wc-code-highlighter>
          <p>
            When an agent opens a list of incidents, the condition becomes part of the query. Records outside their groups are never loaded, so
            they cannot leak through a total, a chart or a search result.
          </p>

          <h3 id='blogpost-cost' class='text-headline-small'>What it costs</h3>
          <p>
            A single check has to be fast, because everything waits for it. We compile each rule once, cache it per role and push conditions
            into the database query instead of filtering rows afterwards.
          </p>
          <figure class='blogpost-quote'>
            <blockquote>
              <p class='text-title-large'>“We stopped asking whether a screen was secure. If it reads data, it went through the check.”</p>
            </blockquote>
            <figcaption class='text-body-medium site-muted'>Samuel Osei, security officer at Northgate Insurance</figcaption>
          </figure>

          <h3 id='blogpost-next' class='text-headline-small'>What comes next</h3>
          <p>
            Next we are adding a way to ask "why can I see this?" for any record, so administrators can follow the rule that allowed it. If you
            want to try it early, <a href='#'>join the waitlist</a>.
          </p>
        </article>

        <aside class='blogpost-aside'>
          <wc-anchor-nav class='blogpost-toc' for='blogpost-body' headline='On this page'></wc-anchor-nav>
        </aside>
      </div>

      <section class='blogpost-related' aria-labelledby='blogpost-related-title'>
        <h3 id='blogpost-related-title' class='text-title-large'>Related posts</h3>
        <ul class='blogpost-grid'>
          {related.map(post => (
            <li>
              <wc-card class='blogpost-card' variant='outlined' href='#'>
                <div class='blogpost-card-media'>
                  <img src={post.image} alt='' width={post.width} height={post.height} loading='lazy' />
                </div>
                <wc-card-content class='blogpost-card-text'>
                  <wc-tag size='sm' accent={post.accent}>{post.topic}</wc-tag>
                  <h4 class='text-title-medium'>{post.title}</h4>
                  <div class='blogpost-card-author'>
                    <wc-avatar name={post.author} size='sm'></wc-avatar>
                    <span class='text-label-large'>{post.author}</span>
                    <time class='text-body-small site-muted' datetime={post.date}>{post.dateLabel}</time>
                  </div>
                </wc-card-content>
              </wc-card>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </section>
    </main>

    <WebsiteFooter />
  </div>
</Layout>
